	// For i=1: dp[1][0][curr][0][0] = 1 for curr in {1,2,3,4} (using 0 as dummy for prev)
	// Actually let's use 1-indexed y values: 1,2,3,4.

	// For i=2: dp[2][prev][curr][0][0] = 1 if prev != curr, for all prev,curr in {1,2,3,4}

	// For i >= 3: when we add next to (i, prev, curr), we check if curr is a peak or valley:
	// - If prev < curr > next: curr is a peak, so p increases by 1
	// - If prev > curr < next: curr is a valley, so v increases by 1
	// - Otherwise: no change to p or v
	// Then new state is (i+1, curr, next, p', v')

	// Final answer: sum over all prev, curr, p=t, v=t-1 of dp[n][prev][curr][t][t-1]

	// Dimensions:
	// i: 1..n (up to 20)
	// prev, curr: 1..4 (4 values each)
	// p: 0..t (up to 10)
	// v: 0..t-1 (up to 9)

	// Use a 5D array or map. Let's use a 5D slice.

	// dp[i][prev][curr][p][v]
	// i from 1 to n
	// prev from 0 to 3 (0 means no prev, i.e., i=1)
	// curr from 0 to 3 (representing y-1)
	// p from 0 to t
	// v from 0 to t-1

	// Let's use 0-indexed for y: 0,1,2,3 representing 1,2,3,4

	maxP := t
	maxV := t - 1

